<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Saxe-Wittenberg</h1><p class="st-meta">1296 CE – 1356 CE · capital Wittenberg</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Ascanian duchy on the middle Elbe that won the Saxon electoral vote in 1356 and became the core of the Electorate of Saxony.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-lion">Henry the Lion</a> in 1180, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> ducal title passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernhard-duke-of-saxony">Ascanian Bernhard</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-anhalt">Anhalt</a>, whose heirs held lands around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wittenberg">Wittenberg</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a> and around <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-saxe-lauenburg">Lauenburg</a> in the north. In 1296 the inheritance was partitioned: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-ii-duke-of-saxony">Albert II</a> kept Wittenberg, while his nephews received the Duchy of Saxe-Lauenburg.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The duchy was small, but its Ascanian dukes claimed the full dignity of the old <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-saxony">Duchy of Saxony</a>, including the office of imperial arch-marshal and a vote in royal elections, which <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-saxe-lauenburg">Lauenburg</a> disputed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudolf-i-duke-of-saxe-wittenberg">Rudolf I</a> was a close ally of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iv">Emperor Charles IV</a>. The territory was agrarian, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wittenberg">Wittenberg</a> a modest residence town at an important <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a> crossing.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-bull-of-1356">Golden Bull of 1356</a> settled the dispute in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wittenberg">Wittenberg's</a> favour, recognising <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudolf-i-duke-of-saxe-wittenberg">Rudolf I</a> as prince-elector and creating the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/electorate-of-saxony">Electorate of Saxony</a>. When the Ascanian line died out in 1422, the electorate passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wettin">Wettin</a> margraves of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meissen">Meissen</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-saxe-wittenberg.jpg" alt="Duchy of Saxe-Wittenberg" width="320" height="267"><figcaption>Image: Gustav Droysen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_Lusatia_and_Saxony_(134037781).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1296 CE – 1356 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Wittenberg</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Albert II, Rudolf I</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Germany</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sachsen-Wittenberg</dd></dl></aside></div>
